# SUBNET MASK & CIDR

## SUBNET MASK
- Defines network vs host part
- Controls network size

## CIDR
- Written as /24, /26 etc
- Number of network bits
- Higher CIDR = smaller network

## CIDR QUICK VALUES
0   → /24
128 → /25
192 → /26
224 → /27
240 → /28
248 → /29
252 → /30

# KEY FORMULAS
- Block size = 256 − last octet
- Usable = block size − 2

# SUBNET EXAMPLES

## Example 1
255.255.255.224
- CIDR: /27
- Block size: 32
- Usable: 30

## Example 2
255.255.255.240
- CIDR: /28
- Block size: 16
- Usable: 14

## Example 3
255.255.255.248
- CIDR: /29
- Block size: 8
- Usable: 6

# SUBNET LOGIC
- Range 0–255 is split into equal blocks
- Block size determines subnet ranges

Example (/26):
0–63
64–127
128–191
192–255

# IMPORTANT RULES
- First IP = Network (not usable)
- Last IP = Broadcast (not usable)
- Middle IPs = usable hosts

